{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/retrieve-references/SKILL.md; cat scientific-evidence/spec.md; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\np=json.load(open('paper_data.json'));print('qualification',[(k,v.get('value')) for k,v in p['grounding_sources'].items() if 'qualification' in k]);print('final metrics',p['final_evaluation']['metrics']);print('sources',list(p['grounding_sources']));print('program locations',[(v.get('program_dir'),v.keys()) for v in p['all_programs']]);s=json.load(open('paper_strategy.json'));print('strategy fields',s.keys());print('title',s.get('paper_title'));print('figures',json.dumps(s['figure_specs'],indent=2));r=json.load(open('references_provenance.json'));print('ref sample',str(r)[:2400])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: retrieve-references\ndescription: Retrieve grounded BibTeX references from Semantic Scholar Graph API, OpenAlex, or Google Scholar (`scholarly`) and write references.bib plus provenance JSON for paper generation.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nFetch references from Semantic Scholar Graph API, OpenAlex, or Google Scholar (via `scholarly`) and keep only real BibTeX entries.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final report/paper generation.\n- Related-work section needs grounded citations.\n\n# What to do\n1. Build focused search queries from task goal, method names, and top experiment ideas.\n2. Preferred path (Semantic Scholar Graph API):\n```bash\npython scripts/s2_fetch.py \\\n  --query \"query one\" \\\n  --query \"query two\" \\\n  --min-refs 8 \\\n  --max-refs 15 \\\n  --out-bib references.bib \\\n  --out-json references_provenance.json\n```\n3. Fallback path (OpenAlex):\n```bash\npython scripts/openalex_fetch.py \\\n  --query \"query one\" \\\n  --query \"query two\" \\\n  --min-refs 8 \\\n  --max-refs 15 \\\n  --search-limit 10 \\\n  --out-bib references.bib \\\n  --out-json references_provenance.json\n```\n   Optional:\n   - `--mailto you@example.com` to enter the OpenAlex polite pool\n   - Env fallback: `OPENALEX_EMAIL`\n4. Alternate path (Google Scholar API wrapper with `scholarly`):\n```bash\npython -m pip install scholarly\npython scripts/scholarly_fetch.py \\\n  --query \"query one\" \\\n  --query \"query two\" \\\n  --min-refs 8 \\\n  --max-refs 15 \\\n  --search-limit 25 \\\n  --sort-by relevance \\\n  --proxy-mode auto \\\n  --delay-seconds 0.5 \\\n  --out-bib references.bib \\\n  --out-json references_provenance.json\n```\n   Proxy options:\n   - `--proxy-mode auto|none|free|single`\n   - `--proxy-http <url>` / `--proxy-https <url>` for `single`\n   - Env fallbacks: `SCHOLARLY_PROXY_MODE`, `SCHOLARLY_PROXY_HTTP`, `SCHOLARLY_PROXY_HTTPS`\n   - Global `HTTP_PROXY` / `HTTPS_PROXY` are intentionally ignored to avoid accidental ambient proxying\n5. Require `S2_API_KEY` when available for `s2_fetch.py` (script reads env automatically).\n6. Keep only references with valid BibTeX and citation key.\n7. Dedupe by citation key and paper id.\n8. Fail hard if references found `< min-refs`.\n9. Use `s2_fetch.py` first for stability, `openalex_fetch.py` as the next fallback, and `scholarly_fetch.py` last if the other sources fail.\n\n# Outputs\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n\n# Notes\n- Use `templates/provenance.schema.json` for expected shape.\n# scientific-evidence\n\nThis is the canonical generated spec bundle for the `scientific-evidence` skill.\nUse the skill script as the source of truth for schemas, layout, and wording.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis contract is generic and file-format-agnostic.\nIt supports inline evidence refs in text-like files, adjacent `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts, and a central JSONL ledger.\n\n## Why this exists\n\nThe point of `scientific-evidence` is to make an agent leave a machine-checkable trail for important numbers, figures, tables, and derived outputs.\nA paper or report should not depend on free-form prose alone when those claims can point back to real artifacts.\nAlways use this contract during paper writing whenever the agent adds manuscript numbers, table values, figure inputs, or derived statistics.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\nAgents should use this layout exactly. Do not invent alternate bundle directories.\n\n## Grammar\n\n```text\nEvidenceRef   := '@evidence[[' Target [ '#' Locator ] ']]'\nTarget        := non-empty path | absolute path | URI | stable identifier\nLocator       := opaque fragment without closing ']]'\nSidecar       := '<artifact>.prov.json'\nLedger        := 'scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl'\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- `Target` stays generic. The verifier may classify it as `path`, `absolute-path`, `uri`, `id`, or `unknown`.\n- `Locator` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Text-like artifacts should use inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs. Binary or generated outputs should carry an adjacent `.prov.json` sidecar.\n\n### Minimal examples\n\n- `@evidence[[TARGET]]`\n- `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `figure.png.prov.json`\n\n## Verification behavior\n\nA verifier uses this bundle to inspect whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\nThe base verifier uses three reference states:\n\n- `resolved`: the target exists and the verifier can validate the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: the target exists, but only path/hash checks were possible or the locator remained opaque.\n- `dangling`: the target does not resolve, the sidecar chain breaks, or the reference is malformed.\n\n### Minimum verifier expectations\n\n1. Resolve every inline evidence ref to a target path, URI, or id when possible.\n2. Mark each ref as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`.\n3. For manifests, require every `derived_from` edge to resolve to at least `anchored`.\n4. When hashes are recorded, compare them. Missing hashes are allowed.\n5. Do not pretend to interpret unknown locator grammars. Keep them `anchored` unless an adapter proves more.\n\n### What verification should prove\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n\n- scan text-like artifacts for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- resolve refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It proves whether the agent left enough structure for later auditing and stricter replay.\nA core paper-writing policy is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should come from real artifacts. Read from files and leave matching `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read or derivation logic.\n\n## Schema set\n\nThe generated bundle includes:\n\n- `schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `spec.md`\n- `bundle.manifest.json`\n- `ledger.jsonl`\n\n### Schema intent\n\n- `evidence-reference`: parsed form of one inline `@evidence[[...]]` token.\n- `artifact-manifest`: canonical sidecar schema for `<artifact>.prov.json`.\n- `ledger-entry`: one JSONL row in `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`.\n- `verification-report`: verifier output with per-reference results and summary counts.\n\n## Examples across file formats\n\n### Python\n\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\n### Bash\n\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\n### LaTeX\n\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\n### Markdown\n\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\n### R\n\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\n### YAML\n\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\n### JSON sidecar\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Prefer paths relative to the scientific-evidence root when practical.\n- Put workflow-specific detail in `payload` or `named_outputs` rather than mutating the base schema.\n- Generate this bundle with the skill script instead of hand-writing downstream copies.\n\n_Generated from scientific-evidence._\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\n\\title{Grounded Experimental Summary}\n\\iclrfinalcopy\n\\author{The Agentic Scientist}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nReplace with a concise grounded abstract summarizing setup, core result, and takeaway.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nReplace with grounded motivation, problem framing, and contributions.\n\n\\section{Related Work}\nSummarize relevant prior work using citation keys present in references.bib (for example, \\citep{templateReference2026}).\n\n\\section{Method}\nDescribe data, setup, and method details needed for reproducibility.\n\n\\section{Results}\nReport grounded quantitative and qualitative findings. Include figures and tables when useful.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nState key findings, limitations, and next steps supported by evidence.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n% REQUIRED: Replace this scaffold with substantive grounded content.\n\\subsection{Reproducibility Details}\nReplace with grounded extended methods, reproducibility details, additional analyses, or supporting tables and figures.\n\n\\end{document}\nqualification [('final.qualification.correct', True), ('final.qualification.outcome', 'qualified'), ('final.qualification.status', 'completed')]\nfinal metrics {'combined_score': 0.9931177679506574, 'control_success_fraction': 1.0, 'failed_episode_fraction': 0.0, 'mean_absolute_capped_normalized_bias': 0.4597691498768297, 'mean_capped_normalized_rmse': 0.6140475146408374}\nsources ['final.evidence.episodes', 'final.metric.combined_score', 'final.metric.control_success_fraction', 'final.metric.failed_episode_fraction', 'final.metric.mean_absolute_capped_normalized_bias', 'final.metric.mean_capped_normalized_rmse', 'final.qualification.correct', 'final.qualification.outcome', 'final.qualification.status', 'result.best_any_score', 'result.best_correct_score', 'result.improvement_001', 'result.improvement_002', 'result.top_program_01', 'result.top_program_02', 'result.total_attempts', 'result.total_correct_programs', 'result.total_evaluated_programs', 'result.total_ideas']\nprogram locations [('work/results/t09/codex/20261107_014043/investigation/ideas/01_endpoint_contrast_audit_with_hidden_spillover_twins/program_1', dict_keys(['attempt_dir_name', 'attempt_id', 'combined_score', 'correct', 'eligible', 'eval_index', 'evaluation_evidence', 'idea_dir_name', 'idea_id', 'program_dir', 'program_id', 'program_idx'])), ('work/results/t09/codex/20261107_014043/investigation/ideas/03_endpoint_contrast_sure_for_exposure_regression/program_1', dict_keys(['attempt_dir_name', 'attempt_id', 'combined_score', 'correct', 'eligible', 'eval_index', 'evaluation_evidence', 'idea_dir_name', 'idea_id', 'program_dir', 'program_id', 'program_idx']))]\nstrategy fields dict_keys(['status', 'selected_prompt_family', 'recommended_title', 'central_question', 'central_thesis', 'narrative_rationale', 'prior_paper_story_status', 'edit_framing_decision', 'staged_input_impact', 'method_groups', 'results_focus', 'figure_plan', 'figure_specs', 'supplementary_plan', 'forbidden_headings', 'forbidden_phrases', 'notes_for_writer', 'failure_reason'])\ntitle None\nfigures [\n  {\n    \"figure_id\": \"endpoint_overview\",\n    \"required\": true,\n    \"purpose\": \"Make the global intervention target and the distinction between observed fitting and endpoint prediction legible at a glance, without asserting empirical calibration of the tuning rule.\",\n    \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n    \"group_by\": \"observed inputs, fitted regression, endpoint contrast\",\n    \"metric\": \"none; conceptual relationships only\",\n    \"split_by\": \"none\",\n    \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n    \"target_width_fraction\": 0.98,\n    \"layout_rationale\": \"The inspected paper.sty is single-column, with 5.5in text width and 9in text height; column width equals full text width, not a 2.6in double-column slot. Target 5.39in width by about 1.7-1.9in height, with caption occupying about one quarter page in total. Place on page 1 or 2. Use one cohesive panel with at most three horizontal zones, at most six short conceptual labels, no tiny equations, and rendered text at least 8.5pt. All-treated/all-control are prediction inputs, not observed outcome labels. Use an editable draw.io source, not invented empirical curves; the task's graph sketch is explicitly schematic.\",\n    \"allow_progression\": false,\n    \"max_panels\": 1,\n    \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n    \"max_series_per_panel\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"figure_id\": \"conditional_accuracy\",\n    \"required\": true,\n    \"purpose\": \"Expose both improvements over assignment-only baselines and regressions relative to one-hop OLS in every final graph-by-condition cell, using final.evidence.episodes only.\",\n    \"figure_type\": \"faceted_dot_whisker\",\n    \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n    \"group_by\": \"baseline comparator; selected estimator is the common numerator\",\n    \"metric\": \"cell RMSE(selected) / cell RMSE(comparator), computed from uncapped final errors; below one favors the selected estimator\",\n    \"split_by\": \"graph family, with six condition rows in each panel\",\n    \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n    \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n    \"layout_rationale\": \"Use a 2-by-2 grid over the inspected 5.5in single text column, about 3.8-4.2in high plus a concise caption, roughly half a body page. Each approximately 2.6in-wide panel has six horizontal condition rows and three dodged comparator markers, a shared logarithmic ratio axis, and a vertical reference at one; use one shared three-entry legend. Labels such as No spill, Weak linear, Strong linear, Threshold, Two-hop, and Cluster are defined in the protocol table. Render labels at least 8.5pt, markers clearly separated, and at most two panels per row. Compute ratios of per-cell RMSEs, not means of per-seed ratios. Seed-block bootstrap intervals are planned summaries, not currently supplied estimates; do not attach significance stars. Report absolute errors beside this figure in a main-text table.\",\n    \"allow_progression\": false,\n    \"max_panels\": 4,\n    \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n    \"max_series_per_panel\": 3\n  },\n  {\n    \"figure_id\": \"diagnostic_limits\",\n    \"required\": true,\n    \"purpose\": \"Show that near-noise-level in-sample residuals can coexist with endpoint error, and summarize the bounded assignment-design contrast without causal attribution to support or tuning.\",\n    \"figure_type\": \"scatter_and_paired_dot_whisker\",\n    \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n    \"group_by\": \"panel A: threshold, two-hop, and remaining conditions; panel B: assignment design\",\n    \"metric\": \"panel A: observed-fit RMSE versus absolute uncapped GATE error; panel B: selected-estimator RMSE under strong linear spillover with individual versus cluster assignment\",\n    \"split_by\": \"two diagnostic questions; panel B has four graph-family categories\",\n    \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n    \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n    \"layout_rationale\": \"Use two panels in one row across the inspected 5.5in single-column text width, approximately 2.4-2.7in high plus caption, about one third page. Panel A uses transparent points and three semantic groups, not six colored mechanism series; show all scientific records and no fitted causal trend. Its observations are seed-dependent and are not independent sample-size evidence. Panel B has four graph categories and two design series with seed-block uncertainty, not four overlapping method curves. Keep labels at least 8.5pt and use the same condition names as Figure 2. If crowding occurs, stack the two panels and revise the page allocation rather than shrinking text; all support-fraction tables remain in the supplement.\",\n    \"allow_progression\": false,\n    \"max_panels\": 2,\n    \"max_categories_per_panel\": 4,\n    \"max_series_per_panel\": 3\n  }\n]\nref sample {'campaign_results_dir': 'work/results/t09/codex/20261107_014043/investigation', 'count': 25, 'generated_at': '2026-11-07T03:34:17.048344+00:00', 'queries': ['\"Regression adjustments for estimating the global treatment effect in experiments with interference\" Chin', '\"Model-based regression adjustment with model-free covariates for network interference\" Han Ugander', 'Stein unbiased risk estimation linear functional ridge regression', 'generalized cross validation ridge regression', '\"Estimating average causal effects under general interference, with application to a social network experiment\" Aronow Samii', '\"Graph cluster randomization: network exposure to multiple universes\" Ugander', '\"Design and analysis of experiments in networks: Reducing bias from interference\" Eckles Karrer Ugander', 'linear model estimable contrasts variance extrapolation', '\"Social structure of Facebook networks\" Traud Mucha Porter', '\"The Network Data Repository with Interactive Graph Analytics and Visualization\" Rossi Ahmed'], 'reference_query_plan': {'architectural_shifts': ['A joint change from fixed one-hop hinge features and fixed ridge strength to a quadratic exposure reference with endpoint-contrast-targeted ridge selection; both remain shallow exposure regressions, and no ablation isolates the two changes', 'A proposed change of tuning objective from observed-outcome prediction to endpoint-functional error; no successful same-reference GCV comparison establishes its isolated benefit', 'No validated neural or multi-hop architecture transition: the nonbacktracking two-hop GCV attempt lacks a valid comparative result'], 'benchmarks': ['Synthetic stochastic block model graphs (SBM), present in development and final evidence', 'Synthetic Barabasi-Albert graphs (BA), present in development and final evidence', 'Synthetic Watts-Strogatz graphs (WS), present in development and final evidence', 'Facebook100 Stanford3 (socfb-Stanford3), present in final evaluation evidence; public graph topology with synthetic experimental outcomes, not a real platform experiment'], 'central_topic': 'Regression-based estimation of the all-treated versus all-control global average treatment effect under network interference, emphasizing endpoint-contrast-targeted ridge tuning, exposure repr\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
